little bit about that?

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:28.830
DONNA MALAYERI: So Cloud Run is
a really interesting product,

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.970
because it offers
Serverless containers.

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:32.770
And it comes in two flavors.

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:34.240
There is a fully
managed version,

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:36.450
which is the one that's
suitable for most users.

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:39.060
And essentially, you
run on Google's compute.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:42.900
So Google becomes your
operations manager essentially.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:46.200
There is also a version
called Cloud Run for Anthos

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.780
if you already have
a Kubernetes cluster,

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:51.570
and you want to take on
more of those operations

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:52.440
responsibilities.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:54.720
So really Cloud Run is
a really unique product

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:56.370
in that it offers
two different ways

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:59.040
to run the product, depending
on what your needs are,

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:00.540
depending on whether
or not you want

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:02.640
to take over operational
responsibilities,

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:05.260
or if you want to hand
those over to Google.

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:07.920
And if you provide any
stateless container,

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:10.450
you can run it on
either platform.

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:13.230
JON FAUST: Can you tell us
why Cloud Run is so important?

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:15.063
And I know that you
mentioned that there are

00:05:15.063 --> 00:05:16.470
two different flavors of it.

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:18.000
Can you tell us a
little bit of why

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.120
Cloud Run is so important
to developers and a lot

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.200
of enterprise customers?

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:22.320
Yeah.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:22.870
Of course.

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:25.620
So one of the pieces of feedback
we got with Google Cloud

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:28.710
Functions was that people
wanted to do Serverless,

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:30.930
but they wanted it
without the restrictions

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.080
of the environment.

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:35.040
So typically with Serverless
products, Cloud Functions,

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:38.040
Lambda, that kind of
thing, you have limitations

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:39.630
on what runtimes are supported.

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:42.330
Because it's essentially
providing a wrapper

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:44.160
around your code,
and you provide just

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:45.600
your function execution.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:48.230
So there are specific
languages that are in use.

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:50.610
You usually can't install
anything in your environment.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:54.460
If you want dependencies, you
have to do a lot of extra work,

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:56.680
especially if they're
native dependencies.

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:58.830
So what we decided
was, hey, how do we

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:00.460
make more things Serverless?

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:03.690
How do we make it if you have an
existing app, such as one that

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:06.480
does server side rendering,
like Python Flask,

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:09.960
or ASP.net, how can you
bring that to a Serverless

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:14.250
environment, and really not have
to rewrite your code entirely?

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:16.270
Use the same exact tools
that you're used to.

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:18.270
So that's why we
based our product

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:21.150
on standard OCI containers.

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:24.210
And essentially you
provide stateless.

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:27.892
Cause you can't depend on
state in between invocations.

00:06:27.892 --> 00:06:29.850
The other thing that's
unique about the product

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:32.960
is that it will reactivate
existing instances.

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:36.150
So unlike functions as a service
products where you can only

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:39.270
have one instance per
execution, here you

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:40.530
can set a concurrency setting.

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:42.330
So it can be one,
which would match

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:44.940
the behavior on a functions
as a service platform.

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:46.830
Or you can set it to
something like 80,

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:49.290
which means that you get
to reuse those instances,

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:53.310
similar to how you would have
a traditional application.

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.500
So it allows you to
be more efficient

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.270
with your compute resources.

00:06:57.270 --> 00:06:59.070
AJA HAMMERLY: So you
mentioned containers.

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:01.968
Do I have to be an expert in
containers to use this tool?

00:07:01.968 --> 00:07:02.760
DONNA MALAYERI: No.

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:03.700
Which is great.

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:05.460
I can tell you,
I literally spent

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:08.100
an hour debugging an issue
in my Docker file once.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:10.890
And I personally find
it kind of challenging

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:12.510
to use Docker files,
especially coming

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:13.980
from a Serverless environment.

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:17.210
Now, the cool thing is,
because OCI containers are

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:20.490
a standard format, there's a
lot of tools that will generate

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:22.140
a Docker container for you.

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:23.880
So there's Jiv for Java.

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:25.080
There's Ko for Go.

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:28.020
And then there's also
the CMCF build packs

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:32.040
project, which is done
by pivotal and Roku.

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:34.920
So essentially, you get
the really nice experience

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:37.170
that you get on a
platform like Roku,

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:39.240
where it looks at your
code, it figures out

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:42.430
what language you're using, and
it builds a container for you.

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:43.978
And when you use a
build pack, that's

00:07:43.978 --> 00:07:45.270
exactly the experience you get.

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:48.038
So you don't even have to author
a Docker file if you're not

00:07:48.038 --> 00:07:49.080
interested in doing that.

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:49.690
AJA HAMMERLY: Hm.

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:50.190
OK.

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:51.900
That sounds much,
much more approachable

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:53.250
than I was imagining.

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:56.400
So Cloud Run is based on
containers rather than

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:59.160
functions and the functions
as a service model

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:02.100
that Google Cloud Functions
and AWS Lambda use.

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:03.930
Can we still count
it as Serverless?

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:04.110
DONNA MALAYERI: Yeah.

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:05.360
That's a really good question.

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:08.340
Because when Lambda came out,
that was the first Serverless

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:09.150
product.

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:11.970
And people started
associating functions

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:13.260
as a service with Serverless.

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:15.450
In fact, people said
Serverless was only compute.

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:18.682
And it has to be, you
provide snippet of code.

00:08:18.682 --> 00:08:21.015
And if you do anything else,
then that's not Serverless.

00:08:21.015 --> 00:08:22.560
The thing is that
in the community,

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:24.330
the definition of
Serverless has evolved.

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:26.760
So people talk about
Serverless databases.

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:29.460
People will talk about something
called serviceful computing,

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:31.740
where you're using more
and more managed services,

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:33.780
rather than running
things yourself,

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:36.480
like if you use a machine
learning service, rather than

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:38.970
training your own model, or
running a machine learning

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:40.230
workload on VM.

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:42.600
So essentially, one way to
think about it when you're

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:45.120
running a container is that
you've taken on a little bit

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:46.600
more responsibility.

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:50.510
You are essentially deciding
what OS packages you want,

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:54.140
what base image you want, and
what runtime you want to use.

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:57.530
So that means that you have
to keep your Docker file

00:08:57.530 --> 00:08:59.520
or whatever you're
using up to date.

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:02.630
So if there is a security
issue in your language runtime,

00:09:02.630 --> 00:09:04.970
you are taking on
that responsibility.

00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:07.430
So there's not
necessarily an option

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:09.002
that's the best for everyone.

00:09:09.002 --> 00:09:10.460
It's really a matter
of trade offs.

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:13.130
If you need to install
custom packages,

00:09:13.130 --> 00:09:15.590
if you have an existing
app that really doesn't

00:09:15.590 --> 00:09:17.900
work in a functions as
a service environment,

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:20.580
then something like Cloud
Run is a really good fit.

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:23.840
If you want to provide
just snippets of code,

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:27.000
and have more of a
managed experience,

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:29.900
then you can use a product
like Google Cloud Functions.

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:33.360
The other thing is that you
can actually mix and match.

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:38.600
So we've put out an open source,
JavaScript Functions Framework.

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:40.490
And essentially
what that does is

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:42.110
it gives you that
same experience

00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:44.420
that you have on Google
Cloud Functions, except you

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:46.040
can run it in a container now.

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:49.790
So you don't have to choose
upfront which version you want

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:52.460
to use if you want to have
a functions experience

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:53.678
or a container experience.

00:09:53.678 --> 00:09:54.470
JON FAUST: Awesome.

00:09:54.470 --> 00:09:56.790
You mentioned
there are sometimes

00:09:56.790 --> 00:09:58.190
several trade offs to using it.

00:09:58.190 --> 00:10:00.860
I'm curious, what are the
pros, the best feature

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:03.100
set that you can probably
give us about Cloud Run?

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:04.310
DONNA MALAYERI: So the
thing that's really unique

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:06.910
is the fact that you can
bring existing applications.

00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:08.750
There is no other
product that offers

00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:10.057
that in a Serverless model.

00:10:10.057 --> 00:10:12.140
There's obviously a lot
of products for running it

00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:13.663
in a serverful way.

00:10:13.663 --> 00:10:15.080
But if you want
to run Serverless,

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:18.170
you typically have to
re-architect your application.

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:19.670
The other thing
that's really unique

00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:22.880
is the fact that you
can reuse instances,

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:24.050
like I was mentioning.

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:27.800
So that means that you have
essentially a very similar

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:31.190
execution environment with
the efficiency that you have

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:33.110
with traditional
compute, except you

00:10:33.110 --> 00:10:35.150
don't have to manage
the scale out yourself.

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:36.850
You don't have to
pay for instances

00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:37.850
when they're not in use.

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:39.050
And you can scale to zero.

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:41.840
So really you should
think of it as bringing

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:44.480
all the benefits of
containers, and the flexibility

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:47.630
that containers provide, and
the open tooling that there

00:10:47.630 --> 00:10:50.900
is for containers along with
the benefits of Serverless.

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:51.650
JON FAUST: Gotcha.

00:10:51.650 --> 00:10:53.870
So for any developer
which is getting

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:56.630
started or considering
using Cloud Run,

00:10:56.630 --> 00:10:59.195
how do you decide whether to
use the fully managed Cloud

00:10:59.195 --> 00:11:01.303
Run, versus the one
that just runs on GKE?

00:11:01.303 --> 00:11:02.970
DONNA MALAYERI: That's
a great question.

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:05.000
So typically what we
found with customers

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:07.172
is as soon as we describe
the two products,

00:11:07.172 --> 00:11:09.380
they know immediately which
product they want to use.

00:11:09.380 --> 00:11:11.060
Most customers, as
I said, want to use

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:12.320
the fully managed product.

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:15.230
Because they don't want
to have the overhead

00:11:15.230 --> 00:11:16.760
of managing a
Kubernetes cluster,

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:18.800
even if they have the expertise.

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:21.890
And if Cloud Run fully
managed meets your needs,

00:11:21.890 --> 00:11:23.930
then definitely
use that product.

00:11:23.930 --> 00:11:26.240
Now, there are a lot of
customers, especially

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:27.860
large enterprises,
who have decided

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:29.720
to standardize on Kubernetes.

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:32.330
Or they have on prem
installations on Kubernetes

00:11:32.330 --> 00:11:33.830
that they want to
move to the cloud.

00:11:33.830 --> 00:11:36.320
And for these customers,
it's a lot more

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:40.280
effective to reuse
their existing cluster

00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:41.720
and their existing tools.

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:43.850
And what these customers
are looking for

00:11:43.850 --> 00:11:45.950
is an easier
developer experience

00:11:45.950 --> 00:11:48.050
so that you don't have
to be a Kubernetes

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:51.170
expert as a developer in
order to deploy applications.

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:54.470
And typically, with these
Kubernetes clusters,

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:57.320
since you have to
pre-provision your capacity,

00:11:57.320 --> 00:11:59.810
you end up having a spare
compute that's not used.

00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:03.140
So Serverless is a great
fit for these applications,

00:12:03.140 --> 00:12:05.990
because it allows
you to scale out

00:12:05.990 --> 00:12:08.550
as your workload
gets more traffic

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:09.620
and then scale back down.

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:13.720
So really it's a more efficient
use of your existing Kubernetes

00:12:13.720 --> 00:12:14.323
cluster.

00:12:14.323 --> 00:12:15.740
One scenario, for
instance, that's

00:12:15.740 --> 00:12:18.440
really resonated with
some customers is

00:12:18.440 --> 00:12:21.620
disaster recovery, where
they have the workload that's

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:25.010
running, say, in an
existing Kubernetes cluster.

00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:27.120
It's taking up some
amount of capacity.

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:30.660
But if that fails, they want
to failover to another cluster.

00:12:30.660 --> 00:12:33.192
And maybe the cluster starts
out with a very small size.

00:12:33.192 --> 00:12:34.150
And then they scale up.

00:12:34.150 --> 00:12:36.510
But essentially, you can
minimize the amount of downtime

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:37.010
you have.

00:12:37.010 --> 00:12:38.630
And it minimizes
the amount of spend

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:40.790
that you have to use for
some compute capacity

00:12:40.790 --> 00:12:41.790
that you may never need.

00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:43.165
JON FAUST: Just
as a follow up, I

00:12:43.165 --> 00:12:45.140
know you mentioned that
most of your customers

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:48.620
seem to want to shift from
being on prem to fully managed.

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:51.200
But what about those that
want to stay on prem?

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:53.697
Is Cloud Run still a tool
that they can still use?

00:12:53.697 --> 00:12:54.530
DONNA MALAYERI: Yes.

00:12:54.530 --> 00:12:58.420
So Anthos gives you a lot
of those capabilities.

00:12:58.420 --> 00:13:00.670
So there's a few
flavors of Anthos.

00:13:00.670 --> 00:13:03.530
Anthos is Google's app
moderization platform.

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:04.540
It's not one product.

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:06.230
It's a suite of products.

00:13:06.230 --> 00:13:08.930
And the idea is
that it's typically

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:10.160
geared towards enterprises.

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:12.470
But as an enterprise, you
have existing investments.

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:15.420
And you want to see how to
leverage those investments.

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:17.450
So if you're running
on premise, on VMware,

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:20.610
we have a version of
Cloud Run that runs there.

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:23.270
So it's a very
similar experience

00:13:23.270 --> 00:13:26.450
to what you would get
on GKE on Kubernetes,

00:13:26.450 --> 00:13:29.190
but you get to run it
on your own hardware.

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:31.843
So we basically
have a few levels.

00:13:31.843 --> 00:13:33.260
We have the fully
managed version,

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:35.540
where you're running on
Google's internal compute.

00:13:35.540 --> 00:13:37.970
It actually runs on Google
Borg, if you've heard of that.

00:13:37.970 --> 00:13:40.970
And Google is basically
your ops team.

00:13:40.970 --> 00:13:43.220
We're obviously going to
make some assumptions on how

00:13:43.220 --> 00:13:44.930
you want your platform to run.

00:13:44.930 --> 00:13:46.910
If those assumptions
don't make sense for you,

00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:48.680
then you can run on
Kubernetes and take on

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:49.730
more responsibility.

00:13:49.730 --> 00:13:51.950
But you still have a
more managed experience,

00:13:51.950 --> 00:13:54.800
where you don't have to think
about the actual hardware

00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:55.800
and then the apps.

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:58.010
Now, if that doesn't
work for you either,

00:13:58.010 --> 00:14:01.340
and you already have a data
center on prem or somewhere

00:14:01.340 --> 00:14:04.280
else, and you want to use
that existing investment,

00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:08.180
then you can use Cloud Run
for Anthos deployed on VMWare.

00:14:08.180 --> 00:14:11.630
So it's really up to you
which one you want to use.

00:14:11.630 --> 00:14:15.480
And the API between all of
these versions is identical.

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:17.030
So if you change
your mind-- you're

00:14:17.030 --> 00:14:19.460
using the fully managed
Cloud Run, for instance,

00:14:19.460 --> 00:14:21.860
and then your company
says, hey, please,

00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:24.020
just put that all on
the Kubernetes cluster,

00:14:24.020 --> 00:14:26.390
or you're using the fully
managed for your dev mode,

00:14:26.390 --> 00:14:29.150
you can easily move
across environments

00:14:29.150 --> 00:14:31.110
without rewriting any code.

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:34.200
And your config actually
mostly will stay the same.

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:37.490
AJA HAMMERLY: So when we
talk about all the Cloud Run

00:14:37.490 --> 00:14:39.470
and Serverless stuff,
KNative comes up.

00:14:39.470 --> 00:14:42.260
Can you talk a little bit about
KNative and its relationship

00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:43.167
to Cloud Run?

00:14:43.167 --> 00:14:44.000
DONNA MALAYERI: Yes.

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:47.420
So KNative is the API that
Cloud Run is based on.

00:14:47.420 --> 00:14:50.090
On the fully managed
version of Cloud Run,

00:14:50.090 --> 00:14:52.860
we don't actually run
a Kubernetes cluster.

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:55.820
But we do have a
Kubernetes API server.

00:14:55.820 --> 00:14:59.240
So the API that you use to
deploy your application,

00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:02.300
regardless of whether you're
using the fully managed Cloud

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:06.170
Run or the version that runs
on GKE, is the KNative API.

00:15:06.170 --> 00:15:08.420
And that's why we
have this portability

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:11.780
between our platforms, and even
portability to other vendors.

00:15:11.780 --> 00:15:16.050
So at Google Cloud Next
in April in San Francisco,

00:15:16.050 --> 00:15:17.960
we had a demo in
the keynote where

00:15:17.960 --> 00:15:21.090
we deployed an application
on the fully managed version,

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:23.480
then the same
application on GKE.

00:15:23.480 --> 00:15:27.930
And then we deployed that as
well on IBM's KNative offering.

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:31.310
So because it's all based
on KNative, it's portable.

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:34.910
And the version of the
service that we run on GKE

00:15:34.910 --> 00:15:38.540
is essentially the OSS version
with some additional GKE

00:15:38.540 --> 00:15:40.640
specific features added on top.

00:15:40.640 --> 00:15:42.020
You can move to other vendors.

00:15:42.020 --> 00:15:43.110
And you can even
change your mind

00:15:43.110 --> 00:15:44.840
and say, hey, I don't
want to even deal

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:46.040
with Google's installation.

00:15:46.040 --> 00:15:47.510
I want to do everything myself.

00:15:47.510 --> 00:15:49.130
I want just raw KNative.

00:15:49.130 --> 00:15:50.460
You can do that as well.

00:15:50.460 --> 00:15:52.252
AJA HAMMERLY: So KNative
is the interaction

00:15:52.252 --> 00:15:54.830
contract folks can rely on when
using the various Cloud Run

00:15:54.830 --> 00:15:55.520
products.

00:15:55.520 --> 00:15:56.000
DONNA MALAYERI: Exactly.

00:15:56.000 --> 00:15:56.917
AJA HAMMERLY: Awesome.

00:15:56.917 --> 00:15:59.480
So I know that there has
been some Twitter discussions

00:15:59.480 --> 00:16:01.430
and changes around
KNative governments.

00:16:01.430 --> 00:16:03.260
Can you tell us a little
more about what's going on,

00:16:03.260 --> 00:16:05.060
and maybe why some of the
decisions that were made

00:16:05.060 --> 00:16:05.560
were made?

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:06.393
DONNA MALAYERI: Yes.

00:16:06.393 --> 00:16:06.930
Of course.

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:09.260
So when we started
the KNative project,

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:10.970
Google was the main contributor.

00:16:10.970 --> 00:16:12.740
We were coming up
with all of the ideas,

00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:13.880
starting all the working groups.

00:16:13.880 --> 00:16:15.860
Google was running every
single working group.

00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:18.415
And then as the product took
off, it was pretty amazing.

00:16:18.415 --> 00:16:19.790
We started getting
more partners.

00:16:19.790 --> 00:16:23.570
We got SAP, Red
Hat, Pivotal, IBM.

00:16:23.570 --> 00:16:26.250
And all these companies were
really eager to contribute.

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:28.490
And so we widened the
steering committee

00:16:28.490 --> 00:16:31.730
to include Pivotal,
Red Hat, and IBM.

00:16:31.730 --> 00:16:34.270
And the feedback that we
heard from both our partners

00:16:34.270 --> 00:16:36.770
on the steering committee, as
well as the community at large

00:16:36.770 --> 00:16:40.500
was, hey, Google isn't the
only contributor anymore.

00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:43.537
Now, Google is 60%
of the contribution.

00:16:43.537 --> 00:16:44.870
Why don't we look at this again?

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:46.670
Why don't we broaden this base?

00:16:46.670 --> 00:16:48.740
So what we've
announced recently,

00:16:48.740 --> 00:16:51.147
with very positive reaction
from the community,

00:16:51.147 --> 00:16:52.730
is that we're going
to move to a model

00:16:52.730 --> 00:16:55.640
where, based on
vendor contributions

00:16:55.640 --> 00:16:57.950
and participation, which
doesn't have to just include

00:16:57.950 --> 00:17:00.890
code contributions-- it
could be other contributions,

00:17:00.890 --> 00:17:03.510
like design documents,
running a working group.

00:17:03.510 --> 00:17:05.520
We want to give people
seats based on that.

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:07.760
And our goal is to make
it so that Google doesn't

00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:10.950
have 60% of ownership anymore.

00:17:10.950 --> 00:17:12.680
It's not the 60% contribution.

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:15.109
Where we have more and more
vendors, more and more people

00:17:15.109 --> 00:17:17.420
taking leadership
positions, and ending up

00:17:17.420 --> 00:17:20.329
with more diversity in
KNative, which really

00:17:20.329 --> 00:17:21.839
helps everybody
in the community,

00:17:21.839 --> 00:17:24.319
both our partners who are
building products, folks

00:17:24.319 --> 00:17:28.280
who are contributing to KNative,
or just directly using KNative.

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:30.440
So for Kubernetes,
for instance, I've

00:17:30.440 --> 00:17:33.470
heard that Google
contributes about 25% now.

00:17:33.470 --> 00:17:36.110
And, of course, when it
started, it was 100% Google.

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:38.480
So we'd like to get
to a model like that,

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:41.600
where we have a very open
model with the community.

00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:43.010
And now, the
announcement that we

00:17:43.010 --> 00:17:45.385
made for the steering committee
is really just the start.

00:17:45.385 --> 00:17:47.427
Some people were like,
hey, this is really vague.

00:17:47.427 --> 00:17:49.100
It was actually by
design to be vague,

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:52.400
because we didn't want to
make decisions on behalf

00:17:52.400 --> 00:17:53.790
of the entire community.

00:17:53.790 --> 00:17:56.437
So today, for instance, we
hosted an Ask Me Anything

00:17:56.437 --> 00:17:58.520
for the steering committee
where people could just

00:17:58.520 --> 00:18:00.812
ask questions and talk about
what was important to them

00:18:00.812 --> 00:18:02.040
for governance.

00:18:02.040 --> 00:18:04.820
So we plan to do this in the
open and talk about, well, now,

00:18:04.820 --> 00:18:06.590
what's the algorithm
for deciding seats?

00:18:06.590 --> 00:18:09.110
How do we say when
people can be appointed

00:18:09.110 --> 00:18:11.200
to the technical
oversight committee?

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:13.610
Let's firm up the requirements
for being a working group

00:18:13.610 --> 00:18:16.260
lead and recognize non
code contributions.

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:18.890
So the goal here
is to be very open

00:18:18.890 --> 00:18:20.680
and to work together
as a community.

00:18:20.680 --> 00:18:23.525
Our goal is not to have this
be a Google controlled project.

00:18:23.525 --> 00:18:25.160
If that were the
case, it would've

00:18:25.160 --> 00:18:26.930
been a lot easier to
just open source this

00:18:26.930 --> 00:18:29.180
and throw it over the
wall, versus what we've

00:18:29.180 --> 00:18:30.710
intended to do,
which is to build

00:18:30.710 --> 00:18:32.690
an open and vibrant community.

00:18:32.690 --> 00:18:34.700
JON FAUST: This community
has grown quite large,

00:18:34.700 --> 00:18:35.330
it seems like.

00:18:35.330 --> 00:18:36.880
And it's safe to
say that Serverless

00:18:36.880 --> 00:18:38.880
is no longer just a fad.

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:41.420
So can you tell us
if Serverless is

00:18:41.420 --> 00:18:43.650
going to be the future
of cloud computing?

00:18:43.650 --> 00:18:45.483
DONNA MALAYERI: So this
is a great question.

00:18:45.483 --> 00:18:48.110
My view is that Serverless is
the future of cloud computing.

00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:49.990
It's going to take a
lot of time for people

00:18:49.990 --> 00:18:51.760
to move legacy workloads.

00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:54.110
My view is also that in 5
or 10 years, when Serverless

00:18:54.110 --> 00:18:56.110
does become more
dominant, we'll actually

00:18:56.110 --> 00:18:57.465
see different products.

00:18:57.465 --> 00:18:58.840
We'll start seeing
products where

00:18:58.840 --> 00:19:00.850
you can run stateful
containers, where

00:19:00.850 --> 00:19:04.060
you can have more aspects
of your application work

00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:05.390
in a Serverless manner.

00:19:05.390 --> 00:19:08.170
Now, the thing that kind
of turns some people off

00:19:08.170 --> 00:19:11.020
when they see a platform like
functions as a service is

00:19:11.020 --> 00:19:13.480
they're like, hey, this
isn't flexible enough for me.

00:19:13.480 --> 00:19:15.130
I have existing investments.

00:19:15.130 --> 00:19:17.350
And a lot of these
platforms are geared

00:19:17.350 --> 00:19:19.540
towards 100% new development.

00:19:19.540 --> 00:19:21.080
And this is a fair point.

00:19:21.080 --> 00:19:23.530
And I think that's where
technologies like Cloud Run

00:19:23.530 --> 00:19:26.050
help, and especially
Cloud Run for Anthos that

00:19:26.050 --> 00:19:29.650
runs on Kubernetes,
because this means that you

00:19:29.650 --> 00:19:31.300
have existing applications.

00:19:31.300 --> 00:19:33.260
You can leverage
those investments.

00:19:33.260 --> 00:19:35.960
So I think that in the
next 5 to 10 years,

00:19:35.960 --> 00:19:38.340
Serverless will become
the dominant matter.

00:19:38.340 --> 00:19:41.440
It kind of will be the default
way of developing applications.

00:19:41.440 --> 00:19:44.760
And more and more applications
will start taking a dependency.

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:47.500
We'll have better ideas of
how to deal with existing

00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:50.680
applications and modernizing
them, and really getting

00:19:50.680 --> 00:19:52.277
the benefits of the cloud.

00:19:52.277 --> 00:19:53.860
When the cloud came
out, it was funny,

00:19:53.860 --> 00:19:55.735
because there was all
this marketing message.

00:19:55.735 --> 00:19:58.600
And the technology
didn't really catch up.

00:19:58.600 --> 00:20:00.597
There was this idea of,
oh, infinite compute.

00:20:00.597 --> 00:20:01.930
You just write your application.

00:20:01.930 --> 00:20:04.450
It scales out to
all these machines.

00:20:04.450 --> 00:20:06.300
The vendor does
everything for you.

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:09.880
And, of course, what they
gave us was essentially

00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:11.820
virtualization over hardware.

00:20:11.820 --> 00:20:12.320
Right?

00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:15.820
In the very beginning,
it was the IT team's job

00:20:15.820 --> 00:20:18.380
that got easier and not
the application developer.

00:20:18.380 --> 00:20:19.990
And it's taken a
long time, but I

00:20:19.990 --> 00:20:22.240
think we're getting to the
point where now application

00:20:22.240 --> 00:20:25.120
developers are really
able to leverage

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:26.170
what the cloud provides.

00:20:26.170 --> 00:20:27.837
And to the point where
they're deploying

00:20:27.837 --> 00:20:29.482
cloud applications themselves.

00:20:29.482 --> 00:20:30.940
And we're seeing
new tools come up,

00:20:30.940 --> 00:20:32.820
like Serverless
framework, Pulumi,

00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:35.755
that make it a lot easier
for application developers

00:20:35.755 --> 00:20:39.512
to now take on some
operations responsibilities.

00:20:39.512 --> 00:20:41.970
AJA HAMMERLY: So this is an
entirely silly question, Donna.

00:20:41.970 --> 00:20:43.360
But this morning
a bunch of us were

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:45.340
sitting around talking
about the weirdest thing

00:20:45.340 --> 00:20:48.370
that we've put in a cloud
container for Cloud Run.

00:20:48.370 --> 00:20:49.990
Someone was talking
about Pascal.

00:20:49.990 --> 00:20:51.760
Someone was talking about COBOL.

00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:53.440
So what's the
weirdest thing you've

00:20:53.440 --> 00:20:57.010
seen someone put in a container
and then run on Cloud Run?

00:20:57.010 --> 00:20:58.660
DONNA MALAYERI: So
a couple of ones.

00:20:58.660 --> 00:21:00.370
One that was really
funny and I just

00:21:00.370 --> 00:21:02.210
didn't expect it
would actually work

00:21:02.210 --> 00:21:04.240
was people took
Libra Office, which

00:21:04.240 --> 00:21:05.670
is like a fork of Open Office.

00:21:05.670 --> 00:21:06.410
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:06.410 --> 00:21:08.270
Which is like a
16-year-old binary.

00:21:08.270 --> 00:21:08.770
Right?

00:21:08.770 --> 00:21:10.603
I mean, it's still being
actively developed.

00:21:10.603 --> 00:21:12.860
But some of that code
in there is pretty old.

00:21:12.860 --> 00:21:14.860
And at Cloud Next,
when we announced

00:21:14.860 --> 00:21:16.900
Cloud Run, we're like,
hey, we're telling you

00:21:16.900 --> 00:21:18.490
you can take
existing applications

00:21:18.490 --> 00:21:19.573
and run them on Cloud Run.

00:21:19.573 --> 00:21:20.230
Let's prove it.

00:21:20.230 --> 00:21:21.830
And the container is huge.

00:21:21.830 --> 00:21:22.930
It's over 1 GB.

00:21:22.930 --> 00:21:25.418
It has all these
dependencies, all these fonts.

00:21:25.418 --> 00:21:26.835
And you can take
that application.

00:21:26.835 --> 00:21:28.863
And so we had a PDF converter.

00:21:28.863 --> 00:21:30.280
The other one that
was interesting

00:21:30.280 --> 00:21:32.290
was my co-worker
is really, really

00:21:32.290 --> 00:21:33.430
into functional languages.

00:21:33.430 --> 00:21:35.570
So he had an app.

00:21:35.570 --> 00:21:38.350
This is Mike [? Helmek. ?] He
did a demo at Google Cloud Next

00:21:38.350 --> 00:21:41.540
where he wrote an app in Elixir,
which I hadn't even heard of,

00:21:41.540 --> 00:21:43.900
which is a functional
language similar to Erlang.

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:47.420
And he wrote a Hangouts
Chat integration using that.

00:21:47.420 --> 00:21:49.890
So that really speaks to the
power of using containers.

00:21:49.890 --> 00:21:50.390
Yeah.

00:21:50.390 --> 00:21:51.460
You can run COBOL.

00:21:51.460 --> 00:21:53.770
Good luck trying to run
COBOL on a functions

00:21:53.770 --> 00:21:54.865
as a service platform.

00:21:54.865 --> 00:21:55.990
That's not going to be fun.

00:21:55.990 --> 00:21:57.470
And, of course,
you're using COBOL.

00:21:57.470 --> 00:21:58.220
So that's not fun.

00:21:58.220 --> 00:22:01.042
But if you have these
existing applications,

00:22:01.042 --> 00:22:03.250
and maybe you don't want to
touch them because nobody

00:22:03.250 --> 00:22:05.810
understands how they work
anymore-- this is very common--

00:22:05.810 --> 00:22:07.600
you don't have to
rewrite your entire app

00:22:07.600 --> 00:22:08.517
to make it Serverless.

00:22:08.517 --> 00:22:10.810
And that's really the power
of what we've offered.

00:22:10.810 --> 00:22:13.780
And that's why it's really
exciting to see the uptake.

00:22:13.780 --> 00:22:16.155
AJA HAMMERLY: So what I'm
taking from this is, Cloud Run,

00:22:16.155 --> 00:22:17.530
not just for
greenfield projects.

00:22:17.530 --> 00:22:17.800
DONNA MALAYERI: Yes.

00:22:17.800 --> 00:22:19.717
AJA HAMMERLY: This is a
totally valid solution

00:22:19.717 --> 00:22:21.850
for getting some of
that nasty legacy code

00:22:21.850 --> 00:22:25.340
that you have in the cloud,
running in a scalable way

00:22:25.340 --> 00:22:27.970
so you're not paying extra when
it's not in use and all that.

00:22:27.970 --> 00:22:28.800
Super cool.

00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:29.800
DONNA MALAYERI: Exactly.

00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:32.373
And sometimes Serverless
folks in the community

00:22:32.373 --> 00:22:33.790
keep talking about
rewriting apps.

00:22:33.790 --> 00:22:37.670
And that kind of frustrates me,
because you have an existing

00:22:37.670 --> 00:22:38.170
app.

00:22:38.170 --> 00:22:39.160
And it's not broken.

00:22:39.160 --> 00:22:43.070
Why would you spend your energy
rewriting that, rather than

00:22:43.070 --> 00:22:45.760
doing something that's going
to move your business forward?

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:48.040
Because your customers
aren't paying you

00:22:48.040 --> 00:22:49.670
for your beautiful
cloud architecture.

00:22:49.670 --> 00:22:50.170
Right?

00:22:50.170 --> 00:22:53.620
They're paying you for, what
are you concretely providing?

00:22:53.620 --> 00:22:55.750
And when Amazon
launched their code,

00:22:55.750 --> 00:22:57.860
it was like this terrible
mess of Perl scripts.

00:22:57.860 --> 00:22:58.360
Right?

00:22:58.360 --> 00:22:59.970
Of course, they improved
that over the years.

00:22:59.970 --> 00:23:02.338
But customers didn't care
that there was Perl scripts.

00:23:02.338 --> 00:23:03.130
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah.

00:23:03.130 --> 00:23:05.877
I like the phrase, I don't care
what the gnomes in my computer

00:23:05.877 --> 00:23:07.960
are doing, as long as they
return the right answer

00:23:07.960 --> 00:23:08.680
consistently.

00:23:08.680 --> 00:23:09.958
DONNA MALAYERI: Exactly.

00:23:09.958 --> 00:23:10.750
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah.

00:23:10.750 --> 00:23:12.500
JON FAUST: So you
mentioned briefly before

00:23:12.500 --> 00:23:14.560
that IT is just going
to get so much easier.

00:23:14.560 --> 00:23:16.940
Does that mean that we
will see IT professionals

00:23:16.940 --> 00:23:19.130
and departments starting
to wear new hats, or less

00:23:19.130 --> 00:23:22.420
of a need to hire IT
professionals for applications

00:23:22.420 --> 00:23:23.630
that scale automatically?

00:23:23.630 --> 00:23:25.755
DONNA MALAYERI: So what I
think is that IT is still

00:23:25.755 --> 00:23:26.660
super important.

00:23:26.660 --> 00:23:28.780
What I've seen among
working with developers

00:23:28.780 --> 00:23:31.120
who are adopting
Serverless applications

00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:35.320
is that they are often
very operationally naive.

00:23:35.320 --> 00:23:37.720
They have always
historically worked

00:23:37.720 --> 00:23:39.730
in a model where they're
writing their app,

00:23:39.730 --> 00:23:41.350
and they're throwing
it over the wall.

00:23:41.350 --> 00:23:43.870
And there's some ops team
that is actually deploying it.

00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:45.760
So application
developers are kind of

00:23:45.760 --> 00:23:49.310
in the dark about best
practices that ops teams

00:23:49.310 --> 00:23:50.940
have built for years and years.

00:23:50.940 --> 00:23:54.180
And they've learned through
trial and error what works best

00:23:54.180 --> 00:23:55.060
and what doesn't.

00:23:55.060 --> 00:23:58.560
So in my discussions with
folks in the DevOps community,

00:23:58.560 --> 00:24:01.590
and folks who are traditionally
coming from an ops background,

00:24:01.590 --> 00:24:03.788
is that most of
them, the ones who

00:24:03.788 --> 00:24:05.580
are interested in up
leveling their skills,

00:24:05.580 --> 00:24:07.440
are really excited
about this change.

00:24:07.440 --> 00:24:11.040
Because now with the cloud
becoming more of the forefront,

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:13.660
ops is becoming more
and more important.

00:24:13.660 --> 00:24:15.570
And it's becoming
more democratized

00:24:15.570 --> 00:24:19.320
in that regular developers are
learning how to do operations.

00:24:19.320 --> 00:24:21.660
And they're learning what
it's like to be on call.

00:24:21.660 --> 00:24:26.280
When I worked at Pulumi,
are writing deployments

00:24:26.280 --> 00:24:27.870
in a repeatable manner.

00:24:27.870 --> 00:24:30.150
And they aren't just clicking
around in the console

00:24:30.150 --> 00:24:33.060
and creating services
that they can't reproduce,

00:24:33.060 --> 00:24:36.493
and they can't redeploy, where
you actually have a format

00:24:36.493 --> 00:24:37.410
that you can redeploy.

00:24:37.410 --> 00:24:41.160
And what we discovered was that
made it so that a lot of folks

00:24:41.160 --> 00:24:43.110
didn't need a dedicated
person to do ops.

00:24:43.110 --> 00:24:45.930
Now, of course, to get bigger,
you need some ops folks.

00:24:45.930 --> 00:24:48.690
But what's happening is that
developers are now learning.

00:24:48.690 --> 00:24:51.630
And so now you have this nice
collaborative environment

00:24:51.630 --> 00:24:53.070
between the dev
and the ops folks.

00:24:53.070 --> 00:24:54.528
You no longer have
this model where

00:24:54.528 --> 00:24:56.982
people are throwing things
over the wall, where they're

00:24:56.982 --> 00:24:59.190
clueless about how something
will work in production,

00:24:59.190 --> 00:25:02.430
where there's friction
between these two departments.

00:25:02.430 --> 00:25:04.920
And the ops folks are
annoyed at the developers

00:25:04.920 --> 00:25:06.880
for destabilizing the system.

00:25:06.880 --> 00:25:08.580
The devs are pissed
off at the ops folks

00:25:08.580 --> 00:25:10.360
for not letting them
deploy fast enough.

00:25:10.360 --> 00:25:11.777
And now we're
getting into a model

00:25:11.777 --> 00:25:13.093
where you're working together.

00:25:13.093 --> 00:25:14.760
You're thinking as a
developer what this

00:25:14.760 --> 00:25:16.080
means, what an outage means.

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:17.990
You're thinking
about the SRE skills.

00:25:17.990 --> 00:25:20.130
Or you're working more
closely with your SREs.

00:25:20.130 --> 00:25:23.730
So what I'm seeing is that for
organizations that are really

00:25:23.730 --> 00:25:27.250
eager to learn what
the cloud can offer,

00:25:27.250 --> 00:25:29.100
there's actually
a really, really

00:25:29.100 --> 00:25:31.620
great organizational
transformation that's

00:25:31.620 --> 00:25:32.175
happening.

00:25:32.175 --> 00:25:34.050
JON FAUST: I did a really
quick Google search

00:25:34.050 --> 00:25:35.675
while you were
answering that question,

00:25:35.675 --> 00:25:38.380
because I haven't looked at job
postings in a really long time.

00:25:38.380 --> 00:25:41.010
And I was curious to see if
there was going to be positions

00:25:41.010 --> 00:25:42.450
for ops engineers.

00:25:42.450 --> 00:25:46.080
And I just searched ops,
just as like a general term.

00:25:46.080 --> 00:25:47.580
And sure enough,
Serverless came up.

00:25:47.580 --> 00:25:49.440
And then eventually,
those postings

00:25:49.440 --> 00:25:51.540
started switching to
Serverless engineers.

00:25:51.540 --> 00:25:55.080
So I guess to kind of answer
my own question as well, I

00:25:55.080 --> 00:25:57.750
did see now there's going to be
more of a shift towards hiring

00:25:57.750 --> 00:25:59.430
Serverless
professionals, as well

00:25:59.430 --> 00:26:00.967
as those who still work in ops.

00:26:00.967 --> 00:26:01.800
DONNA MALAYERI: Yes.

00:26:01.800 --> 00:26:03.508
And, in fact, that's
a really good point,

00:26:03.508 --> 00:26:06.440
because one of the first
uses for AWS Lambda,

00:26:06.440 --> 00:26:10.080
and still to this day,
was for operations tasks.

00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:12.330
So it was things like a
virtual machine has started up

00:26:12.330 --> 00:26:13.800
in this AWS account.

00:26:13.800 --> 00:26:15.300
Does it have the
right permission?

00:26:15.300 --> 00:26:16.720
Does it have firewalls set up?

00:26:16.720 --> 00:26:19.410
And if not, tear down
that machine immediately.

00:26:19.410 --> 00:26:21.630
So those were the kinds
of automation tasks

00:26:21.630 --> 00:26:24.450
that were very, very
popular with AWS Lambda.

00:26:24.450 --> 00:26:26.340
And I personally talked
to some ops folks

00:26:26.340 --> 00:26:28.410
who were like, I didn't
know how to use Python.

00:26:28.410 --> 00:26:29.820
I only wrote some bash scripts.

00:26:29.820 --> 00:26:33.810
But this has been such a
useful way to automate my job.

00:26:33.810 --> 00:26:35.730
So tools like Lambda
and Serverless

00:26:35.730 --> 00:26:37.230
aren't just for developers.

00:26:37.230 --> 00:26:38.580
They're for everybody.

00:26:38.580 --> 00:26:38.870
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah.

00:26:38.870 --> 00:26:40.328
One of the talks
I gave this summer

00:26:40.328 --> 00:26:43.380
was about how don't be
afraid of the Serverless.

00:26:43.380 --> 00:26:46.260
And it lets ops folks
focus on the hard

00:26:46.260 --> 00:26:49.710
and the interesting problems,
and make some of the tedium

00:26:49.710 --> 00:26:52.380
and boredom, which our Azure
team here at Google calls toil,

00:26:52.380 --> 00:26:53.140
go away.

00:26:53.140 --> 00:26:55.155
And I think that is so
cool and so powerful.

00:26:55.155 --> 00:26:56.280
DONNA MALAYERI: Absolutely.

00:26:56.280 --> 00:26:58.510
AJA HAMMERLY: So I think we're
running a little short on time.

00:26:58.510 --> 00:27:01.052
Is there anything that we missed
or that you want to mention?

00:27:01.052 --> 00:27:03.580
DONNA MALAYERI: If the term
Serverless is scary to you,

00:27:03.580 --> 00:27:05.855
or if you feel like it's
too hard of a cliff,

00:27:05.855 --> 00:27:07.980
and you want to stay in
the safety of your existing

00:27:07.980 --> 00:27:09.990
applications, you don't
need to be worried.

00:27:09.990 --> 00:27:12.720
Don't be scared by
folks who are doing

00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:14.880
really weird things
with AWS Lambda

00:27:14.880 --> 00:27:16.170
and are the cutting edge.

00:27:16.170 --> 00:27:18.022
Because you're always
going to see people

00:27:18.022 --> 00:27:19.230
doing the cutting edge stuff.

00:27:19.230 --> 00:27:23.010
I think Serverless has a lot
to offer existing applications.

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:24.900
And you don't need
to rewrite your code

00:27:24.900 --> 00:27:27.353
to leverage these benefits.

00:27:27.353 --> 00:27:28.770
AJA HAMMERLY: So
is there anywhere

00:27:28.770 --> 00:27:29.940
that you're going
to be if people

00:27:29.940 --> 00:27:31.815
who listen to the podcast
want to see you out

00:27:31.815 --> 00:27:33.030
in the community?

00:27:33.030 --> 00:27:33.863
DONNA MALAYERI: Yes.

00:27:33.863 --> 00:27:36.930
So I'll be speaking at
Google Cloud Next in London.

00:27:36.930 --> 00:27:41.340
And I did a keynote and
a talk at Serverless Conf

00:27:41.340 --> 00:27:42.330
in New York City.

00:27:42.330 --> 00:27:44.447
And those videos will
be available soon.

00:27:44.447 --> 00:27:46.530
So I recommend checking
those out, as well as some

00:27:46.530 --> 00:27:47.700
of the other speakers there.

00:27:47.700 --> 00:27:49.658
There were some really
interesting talks there.

00:27:49.658 --> 00:27:51.870
AJA HAMMERLY: And if folks
want to find you online,

00:27:51.870 --> 00:27:52.730
are you on Twitter?

00:27:52.730 --> 00:27:55.030
Is there a good place
for them to look you up?

00:27:55.030 --> 00:27:55.863
DONNA MALAYERI: Yes.

00:27:55.863 --> 00:27:57.840
I'm on Twitter @LindyDonna.

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:01.980
And I post about all kinds of
things, including cat photos.

00:28:01.980 --> 00:28:03.474
AJA HAMMERLY: Ooh, cat photos.

00:28:03.474 --> 00:28:04.470
[MEOW]

00:28:04.470 --> 00:28:05.370
JON FAUST: Well, that's
going to wrap it up

00:28:05.370 --> 00:28:06.870
for this episode of the podcast.

00:28:06.870 --> 00:28:08.368
Donna, thank you for joining us.

00:28:08.368 --> 00:28:09.660
It's been an absolute pleasure.

00:28:09.660 --> 00:28:11.110
Aja as well.

00:28:11.110 --> 00:28:13.470
And we really hope to hear
of all the great things

00:28:13.470 --> 00:28:15.310
that Serverless is going to
be doing in the near future.

00:28:15.310 --> 00:28:16.020
DONNA MALAYERI: Thank you.

00:28:16.020 --> 00:28:17.480
AJA HAMMERLY: That was so cool.

00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:19.560
Donna clarified a
whole bunch of stuff

00:28:19.560 --> 00:28:21.392
that I hadn't quite
sorted out for myself.

00:28:21.392 --> 00:28:23.100
So I'm really glad we
got to talk to her.

00:28:23.100 --> 00:28:26.550
And her passion for that
area is so infectious.

00:28:26.550 --> 00:28:28.665
But now it is time for
Question of the Week.

00:28:28.665 --> 00:28:31.635
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:28:34.120 --> 00:28:36.520
So Jon, I know you've been
digging into Open Match,

00:28:36.520 --> 00:28:38.680
the open source
matchmaking for games

00:28:38.680 --> 00:28:41.330
project that a bunch of folks
are working on right now.

00:28:41.330 --> 00:28:42.610
Can you tell me a
little bit about where

00:28:42.610 --> 00:28:45.025
I could learn how to write a
matchmaking function for Open

00:28:45.025 --> 00:28:45.520
Match?

00:28:45.520 --> 00:28:45.700
JON FAUST: Yeah.

00:28:45.700 --> 00:28:46.390
Sure.

00:28:46.390 --> 00:28:50.650
So as of today or yesterday,
the 0.8 release candidate

00:28:50.650 --> 00:28:51.520
has been released.

00:28:51.520 --> 00:28:54.580
So on that release candidate,
you will see a lot of notes

00:28:54.580 --> 00:28:57.400
and getting started guides
on creating a matchmaking

00:28:57.400 --> 00:28:59.710
function, setting up
the director, which

00:28:59.710 --> 00:29:03.550
will allow you to connect
your players to servers,

00:29:03.550 --> 00:29:06.040
as well as messing around
with your front end.

00:29:06.040 --> 00:29:08.765
So the release candidate has a
lot of getting started guides.

00:29:08.765 --> 00:29:10.390
So to answer your
question specifically

00:29:10.390 --> 00:29:12.910
about how to write a matchmaking
function, what you're

00:29:12.910 --> 00:29:17.260
going to do is, we have a small
template on what a matchmaking

00:29:17.260 --> 00:29:18.100
function looks like.

00:29:18.100 --> 00:29:19.210
You have to file a ticket.

00:29:19.210 --> 00:29:21.250
And then you pretty
much submit a proposal.

00:29:21.250 --> 00:29:23.680
And then once your
proposal is accepted,

00:29:23.680 --> 00:29:25.300
you're returned a match.

00:29:25.300 --> 00:29:26.990
And when you get
returned that match,

00:29:26.990 --> 00:29:29.230
then the director will
connect you to your server.

00:29:29.230 --> 00:29:32.465
And after you've written this
function using the template,

00:29:32.465 --> 00:29:34.090
or maybe if you want
to play around it,

00:29:34.090 --> 00:29:35.830
you can alter it
as you like, you

00:29:35.830 --> 00:29:38.080
are going to build
a match make image.

00:29:38.080 --> 00:29:39.700
And then when you
push that image,

00:29:39.700 --> 00:29:42.400
then everything is
easy peasy after that.

00:29:42.400 --> 00:29:44.950
You're going to push it to
your configured registry

00:29:44.950 --> 00:29:49.062
that you have associated with
your Open Match image as well.

